---
/*
  THE CART (2026-08-16, section 129) — the OWNER's call, and it replaces the
  order page section 126 built.

  WHAT HE ASKED FOR, in his words: choose a size on a Creature's page and add it
  to a cart; a cart icon in the header, always visible, with a count; and from
  there a normal checkout — contact details, submit, the order arrives by email.
  No payment, exactly as before. He has seen the page that listed every piece at
  once and does not want it. That page is deleted rather than left standing.

  WHAT A CART COSTS ON THIS SITE, stated plainly because it is the whole reason
  this page is shaped the way it is. A cart is per-visitor mutable state that
  has to survive navigation. This site is static HTML on a CDN: there is no
  session, no per-request render and no database. That leaves three ways to hold
  such state, and two are not available here:

  - LOCALSTORAGE. Needs JavaScript. Every page stays static.
  - A COOKIE READ BY THE SERVER. Needs every cart-bearing page rendered per
    request, which means routing the whole site through a Function and giving up
    the static CDN model, the deploy hook and the URL. That is an infrastructure
    decision, not a shopping-basket one.
  - THE URL. Would work with no JavaScript, but only if every "add" link on
    every page already carried the current basket — and a static build cannot
    know a visitor's basket when it writes those links.

  So: A CART REQUIRES JAVASCRIPT, and so does a count that follows a reader from
  page to page. There is no version of either without it, short of changing how
  the site is served. The owner was told this before it was built and accepted
  it.

  WHICH PAGES STOP WORKING WITHOUT A SCRIPT, and the answer is one — THIS ONE.
  With scripts off it says so and sends the reader to the catalogue, where every
  Creature still has its own order form, which is a plain HTML form that posts
  to the same endpoint and has never needed a script. Nothing else on the site
  changed: the menu is a <details>, the catalogue filter is CSS, every other
  page renders, navigates and posts exactly as it did. The cart control in the
  chrome and the add control on a piece are HIDDEN rather than dead in that
  case, because a control that cannot do its job is worse than no control.

  PAYMENT IS NOT HERE, and the form says so. It collects who is buying and what
  they want and sends it to the brand; money is settled in the reply, when he
  has a fiscal position for it. It posts to the SAME endpoint as every other
  order, behind the SAME gate: with the Resend bindings unset the endpoint
  answers 503 and says sending is not switched on. Nothing leaves this site
  until the privacy notice exists.

  THE PRICES ARE THE BUILD'S, NEVER THE FORM'S. Every figure on this page comes
  from the catalogue below, written by the build from the same query the shop
  renders. The total the reader sees is a convenience computed from those
  figures; the AUTHORITATIVE total is computed by the endpoint from
  /order-catalogue.json, from the same function, because a total computed from
  what arrives in a POST is a total the buyer wrote.
*/
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import TextSurface from "../../components/TextSurface.astro";
import {getGarments} from "../../lib/content";
import {isOrderable, toOrderItem} from "../../lib/order";
import {imageUrl} from "../../lib/image";
import {LOCALES, localePath, toLocale} from "../../lib/locales";
import {t} from "../../i18n/ui";

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return LOCALES.map((lang) => ({params: {lang}}));
}

const locale = toLocale(Astro.params.lang);
const garments = await getGarments();

/*
  THE WHOLE ORDERABLE CATALOGUE, EMBEDDED. The cart holds slugs; this is what
  turns a slug into a name, a price, the sizes he cuts and how many exist. It is
  the same list `/order-catalogue.json` serves, from the same function, plus the
  two things a page needs and a till roll does not: where the piece lives and
  what it looks like.

  A PIECE THAT LEAVES THE SHOP LEAVES THE CART. If he marks something sold or
  takes its price off, it is absent here, the line silently disappears from a
  reader's cart, and the endpoint would have refused to price it anyway. That is
  the correct direction to fail in: the alternative is a cart that keeps
  offering something the brand cannot sell.
*/
const items = garments.filter(isOrderable).map((g) => {
  const [frame] = g.media ?? [];
  return {
    ...toOrderItem(g),
    href: localePath(locale, `creature/${g.slug}`),
    /* small: a cart line is a check that you chose the right object, not a view */
    image: frame ? imageUrl(frame.poster, 240, 62) : null,
  };
});

const catalogueJson = JSON.stringify(items).replace(/</g, "\\u003c");

/*
  ONE PLACE THE CURRENCY IS DECIDED, and it follows the pieces rather than the
  page: every Creature carries its own, they are all EUR today, and hard-coding
  the symbol would be a lie the first time one is not.
*/
const currency = items[0]?.currency ?? "EUR";
---

<Base locale={locale} title={t(locale, "cart")}>
  <TextSurface class="flex flex-col gap-u5 pt-u13 pb-u8">
    <h1 class="type-display text-display">{t(locale, "cart")}</h1>
  </TextSurface>

  <TextSurface class="pb-u21">
    {/*
      WITH NO SCRIPT THIS IS THE WHOLE PAGE. It is not a fallback bolted on
      afterwards: it is what the document says before anything runs, and the
      script hides it by putting `js` on <html> in the head. So a reader with
      scripts off never sees a control appear and then fail — they see a
      sentence and a way on.
    */}
    <div class="cart-noscript">
      <p class="type-body text-body max-w-prose">{t(locale, "cartNeedsScript")}</p>
      <a
        href={localePath(locale, "creature")}
        class="type-label text-body hairline-under self-start"
      >
        {t(locale, "allCreatures")}
      </a>
    </div>

    {/* Written by the script, from localStorage, against the catalogue above. */}
    <div class="cart-live flex flex-col gap-u8" hidden>
      <div data-cart-empty class="flex flex-col gap-u5" hidden>
        <p class="type-body text-body max-w-prose">{t(locale, "cartEmpty")}</p>
        <a
          href={localePath(locale, "creature")}
          class="type-label text-body hairline-under self-start"
        >
          {t(locale, "allCreatures")}
        </a>
      </div>

      <form
        method="POST"
        action="/api/enquiry"
        class="flex flex-col gap-u8"
        data-cart-form
        hidden
      >
        <p class="type-body text-body max-w-prose">{t(locale, "cartIntro")}</p>

        <h2 class="type-label text-label">{t(locale, "cartPieces")}</h2>
        <ul class="flex flex-col gap-u5" data-cart-list></ul>

        {/*
          THE TOTAL IS A CONVENIENCE, NOT A PRICE. It adds up the figures printed
          beside it, all of which came out of the build. The authoritative figure
          is computed by the endpoint from /order-catalogue.json, which a buyer
          cannot edit. `aria-live` so a screen reader hears it change rather than
          having to go and look.
        */}
        <p class="type-mono text-mono flex gap-u3">
          <span>{t(locale, "cartTotal")}</span>
          <output data-cart-total aria-live="polite"></output>
        </p>

        <h2 class="type-label text-label">{t(locale, "cartYourDetails")}</h2>

        <div class="field">
          <label class="type-label text-label" for="name">{t(locale, "yourName")}</label>
          <input id="name" name="name" type="text" required maxlength="120" autocomplete="name" />
        </div>

        <div class="field">
          <label class="type-label text-label" for="email">Email</label>
          <input id="email" name="email" type="email" required maxlength="254" autocomplete="email" />
        </div>

        <div class="field">
          <label class="type-label text-label" for="note">{t(locale, "note")}</label>
          <textarea id="note" name="note" rows="4" maxlength="2000" />
        </div>

        <p class="type-body text-body max-w-prose">{t(locale, "deliveryLine")}</p>
        <p class="type-body text-body max-w-prose">{t(locale, "cartNoPayment")}</p>

        {/* Hidden from people, not from bots: they fill it in, humans cannot. */}
        <div class="spam-trap" aria-hidden="true">
          <label for="website">Website</label>
          <input id="website" name="website" type="text" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" />
        </div>

        <input type="hidden" name="lang" value={locale} />
        <input type="hidden" name="renderedAt" value={String(Date.now())} />

        <button
          type="submit"
          class="type-label text-body hairline-under self-start"
          data-sending={t(locale, "sending")}
        >
          {t(locale, "cartSend")}
        </button>
      </form>
    </div>
  </TextSurface>

  <script
    type="application/json"
    data-cart-catalogue
    is:inline
    set:html={catalogueJson}
  />

  <script
    is:inline
    define:vars={{
      locale,
      currency,
      labels: {
        size: t(locale, "chooseSize"),
        quantity: t(locale, "cartQuantity"),
        remove: t(locale, "cartRemove"),
        onePiece: t(locale, "cartOnePiece"),
      },
    }}
  >
    (() => {
      const live = document.querySelector(".cart-live");
      const form = document.querySelector("[data-cart-form]");
      const list = document.querySelector("[data-cart-list]");
      const empty = document.querySelector("[data-cart-empty]");
      const totalOut = document.querySelector("[data-cart-total]");
      const source = document.querySelector("[data-cart-catalogue]");
      if (!live || !form || !list || !empty || !source) return;

      const KEY = "ac-cart";
      const read = () => {
        try {
          const v = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(KEY) || "{}");
          return v && typeof v === "object" ? v : {};
        } catch {
          return {};
        }
      };
      const write = (v) => {
        try {
          localStorage.setItem(KEY, JSON.stringify(v));
        } catch {
          /* private mode: the page still works for this visit, it just forgets */
        }
        document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent("ac-cart-changed"));
      };

      /*
        THE PAGE MUST NOT GO BLANK IF ANY OF THIS FAILS. The no-script sentence
        is hidden the moment scripts run, and the live block stays hidden until
        this script shows it — so one error would leave a heading over an empty
        page with no way on. Putting the sentence back is not a lie in that
        state: the cart really does not work, and where to go instead is the
        same answer either way.
      */
      const fail = (error) => {
        document.documentElement.classList.remove("js");
        live.hidden = true;
        console.error("[cart]", error);
      };

      let catalogue;
      try {
        catalogue = new Map(JSON.parse(source.textContent).map((i) => [i.slug, i]));
      } catch (error) {
        fail(error);
        return;
      }
      const fmt = new Intl.NumberFormat(locale === "it" ? "it-IT" : "en-GB", {
        style: "currency",
        currency,
        maximumFractionDigits: 0,
      });

      const el = (tag, className, text) => {
        const node = document.createElement(tag);
        if (className) node.className = className;
        if (text != null) node.textContent = text;
        return node;
      };

      /*
        THE LINES ARE BUILT AS NODES, NOT AS A STRING OF HTML. Every name here
        comes out of the dataset, which is the owner's to write; building the
        DOM means no piece can ever be named something that ends up parsed as
        markup, and there is no escaping rule for a later session to get wrong.
      */
      function line(slug, entry) {
        const item = catalogue.get(slug);
        if (!item) return null;

        const li = el("li", "cart-line");
        li.dataset.slug = slug;

        const left = el("div", "flex items-start gap-u3");
        if (item.image) {
          const img = document.createElement("img");
          img.src = item.image;
          img.alt = "";
          img.width = 60;
          img.height = 80;
          img.loading = "lazy";
          img.decoding = "async";
          img.style.objectFit = "cover";
          left.append(img);
        }

        const naming = el("div", "flex flex-col gap-u1");
        const link = el("a", "type-label text-label hairline-under self-start", item.name);
        link.href = item.href;
        naming.append(link, el("span", "type-mono text-mono", fmt.format(item.price)));
        if (item.max === 1) naming.append(el("span", "type-label text-label", labels.onePiece));
        left.append(naming);

        const controls = el("div", "flex flex-wrap items-end gap-u5");

        /* A piece he cuts in one size has nothing to choose (section 101). */
        const sizes = (item.sizes || []).filter((s) => s !== "ONE");
        if (sizes.length > 0) {
          const set = el("fieldset", "flex flex-col gap-u2");
          set.append(el("legend", "type-label text-label", labels.size));
          const row = el("div", "flex flex-wrap gap-u3");
          for (const size of sizes) {
            const label = el("label", "type-label text-label flex items-baseline gap-u1");
            const radio = document.createElement("input");
            radio.type = "radio";
            radio.name = `size:${slug}`;
            radio.value = size;
            radio.checked = entry.size === size;
            label.append(radio, document.createTextNode(size));
            row.append(label);
          }
          /* Nothing stored, or a size he no longer cuts: take the first. */
          if (!row.querySelector("input:checked")) row.querySelector("input").checked = true;
          set.append(row);
          controls.append(set);
        }

        const qtyField = el("div", "field");
        const qtyLabel = el("label", "type-label text-label", labels.quantity);
        qtyLabel.htmlFor = `qty-${slug}`;
        const qty = document.createElement("input");
        qty.id = `qty-${slug}`;
        qty.name = `qty:${slug}`;
        qty.type = "number";
        qty.inputMode = "numeric";
        qty.min = "1";
        /* A 1 of 1 stops at one, because one is all there is. */
        qty.max = String(item.max);
        qty.step = "1";
        qty.value = String(Math.min(Math.max(Number(entry.qty) || 1, 1), item.max));
        qtyField.append(qtyLabel, qty);
        controls.append(qtyField);

        const remove = el("button", "type-label text-label hairline-under", labels.remove);
        remove.type = "button";
        remove.dataset.removeSlug = slug;
        controls.append(remove);

        li.append(left, controls);
        return li;
      }

      function render() {
        const cart = read();
        list.replaceChildren();
        for (const [slug, entry] of Object.entries(cart)) {
          const node = line(slug, entry || {});
          if (node) list.append(node);
        }
        /*
          AND THEN WRITE BACK WHAT WAS BUILT, which is not a tidy-up: the lines
          above CLAMP. A stored quantity of 99 against a 1 of 1 renders as 1 and
          left storage saying 99, so the cart page showed one piece and the count
          in the chrome said 102 — measured, not imagined. A piece he has since
          sold or unpriced is dropped the same way and has to leave storage with
          it, or it comes back on the next page.

          What the reader sees is the truth here, so the truth is taken from the
          rendered lines rather than from what was stored.
        */
        sync(true);
        const has = list.children.length > 0;
        form.hidden = !has;
        empty.hidden = has;
        live.hidden = false;
      }

      /*
        WHAT THE READER TYPES IS WHAT IS KEPT. The controls in the list ARE the
        form fields, so there is nothing to copy across at submit time; this only
        mirrors them back into storage, so the count in the chrome agrees and the
        cart is still theirs on the next page.
      */
      function sync(correct) {
        const next = {};
        let total = 0;
        for (const li of list.querySelectorAll(".cart-line")) {
          const slug = li.dataset.slug;
          const item = catalogue.get(slug);
          if (!item) continue;
          const field = li.querySelector("input[type=number]");
          let qty = Math.floor(Number(field.value));
          if (!Number.isFinite(qty) || qty < 1) qty = 1;
          if (qty > item.max) qty = item.max;
          /*
            THE BOX IS CORRECTED WHEN THE READER LEAVES IT, NOT WHILE THEY TYPE.
            Clearing the box to type "12" empties it for one keystroke; writing
            "1" back into it at that instant fights the person using it. The
            total and the stored cart always use the clamped number, so nothing
            downstream ever sees the half-typed one.
          */
          if (correct && String(qty) !== field.value) field.value = String(qty);
          const size = li.querySelector("input[type=radio]:checked")?.value;
          next[slug] = size ? {qty, size} : {qty};
          total += qty * item.price;
        }
        write(next);
        if (totalOut) totalOut.textContent = fmt.format(total);
      }

      form.addEventListener("input", () => sync(false));
      form.addEventListener("change", () => sync(true));

      list.addEventListener("click", (event) => {
        const button = event.target.closest("[data-remove-slug]");
        if (!button) return;
        const cart = read();
        delete cart[button.dataset.removeSlug];
        write(cart);
        render();
      });

      /*
        THE SENDING STATE, the same enhancement every other form on this site
        has: the browser navigates on submit, and on a slow connection nothing
        appears to have happened, so a person presses again. The button is not
        disabled — a disabled control leaves the accessibility tree mid-action.
      */
      form.addEventListener("submit", () => {
        const button = form.querySelector("button[type=submit]");
        if (!button) return;
        button.setAttribute("aria-busy", "true");
        button.textContent = button.dataset.sending;
      });

      try {
        render();
      } catch (error) {
        fail(error);
      }
    })();
  </script>
</Base>
